# 32-bit Wine on a bootc/composefs root.
#
# THE PROBLEM. Running any 32-bit Windows PE under Wine fails on this image with:
#
#   avc: denied { execmod } for comm="cmd.exe"
#        tcontext=system_u:object_r:lib_t:s0 tclass=file
#
# 64-bit PE binaries are unaffected. 32-bit PE DLLs need text relocations, so Wine
# maps their .text writable-then-executable, which is the `execmod` permission.
#
# WHY THE OBVIOUS EXPLANATIONS ARE WRONG. Wine itself runs as unconfined_t
# (verified with ps -Z), and selinuxuser_execmod is already on — so the accessing
# process is permitted. The denial is reported against kernel_t, which is not any
# user domain, and no user-facing boolean affects it.
#
# The reason is composefs. `/` here is an overlay whose data lives in the ostree
# object store (note the denied path is an object path like /d8/a9f1....file).
# Overlayfs performs the backing-file permission check with the MOUNTER's
# credentials, and the bootc-mounted root is mounted by kernel_t. So the subject of
# the check is the mounter, not Wine. This is why 32-bit Wine works on ordinary
# Fedora and fails here: it is a property of the image's root filesystem.
#
# Corollary: adding wine-core.i686 back does NOT fix this, though SETUP §9 once
# suggested trying that first. The denial is on the PE files' label, not the loader.
#
# SCOPE. This grants execmod on lib_t, i.e. all of /usr/lib64, rather than only
# Wine's i386-windows directory. That breadth is not desired — it is forced. The
# narrow fix is to relabel just Wine's 32-bit PE files to a dedicated type, but
# /usr is read-only composefs, so no runtime chcon/restorecon can apply a label
# there; it has to be done at image build. Until then this is the working fix.
module kb3lyb-wine32 1.0;

require {
    type kernel_t;
    type lib_t;
    class file execmod;
}

allow kernel_t lib_t:file execmod;
